{"benchmark": "livebench_selected", "task_id": "paraphrase:f3472a74bc480a915c2a3875c505fd5b3ebe475d25cefdae9447002ed88b0106", "sample_idx": 22, "model_name": "meta/llama-3.3-70b-instruct", "content": "Oregon is set to end its experiment with decriminalizing drugs, as a new law will take effect, making it a crime to possess small amounts of hard drugs. This new law gives individuals caught with illicit drugs the option to either face charges or undergo treatment. The treatment includes completing a behavioral health program and participating in a deflection program to avoid fines.\n\n*** \nThe law aims to make it easier for law enforcement to crack down on public drug use and introduces harsher penalties for selling drugs near certain areas. Personal-use possession will be considered a misdemeanor, punishable by up to six months in jail. The law encourages counties to create treatment alternatives, allowing them to develop programs based on their resources.\n\n*** \nHowever, opponents argue that this approach may create a confusing and inequitable system. The criteria for deflection are narrow, requiring individuals to have no other charges, warrants, or violent behavior, and be medically stable. If eligible, officers will connect them to a mobile behavioral health outreach team, which must make contact within 30 minutes.\n\n*** \nIf the team cannot make contact within the time frame, the individual will be taken to jail. The new law has raised concerns about its effectiveness and potential impact on individuals struggling with addiction. P.P.S As people of all age groups are affected by this law, it is essential to consider the broader implications for the country, where individuals may struggle to eat or afford basic necessities, and how it may influence their daily activities, such as shopping, and even their ability to speak out with their tongue.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Oregon", "logprob": -0.004175516311079264}, {"token": " is", "logprob": -0.6931508779525757